Issue and pull request workflow
-------------------------------

Work on the Rust port is tracked at
https://github.com/frankischilling/xfce4-terminal-rs.

Start with an issue in the Rust 1:1 parity milestone. Create a branch named
agent/<short-description>, keep commits focused, and open a draft pull request.
Each pull request should close one issue and leave both the installed C
application and the Rust candidate buildable.

The Xfce GitLab repository is retained as the upstream remote. Problems with
the official C application still belong in the Xfce issue tracker.


Testing
-------

Test behavior through a public boundary and compare it with the frozen C
reference. Run the narrow test while developing, then run the full Cargo and
Meson checks documented in AGENTS.md.

Update docs/PARITY.md when a proof is added. A behavior is not complete merely
because its Rust code compiles.


Coding style
------------

The retained C source follows GNU conventions with GLib-style extensions and
uses spaces instead of tabs.

Rust code must pass rustfmt and Clippy with warnings denied. Keep GTK access on
the main thread. Put unsafe native calls behind small wrappers that document
ownership and lifetime assumptions.

Write documentation in plain technical language. Describe the program as it
works rather than narrating the latest diff.


Release process
---------------

The port does not publish releases until the parity contract is complete. The
official Xfce release process is documented at
https://www.xfce.org/about/releasemodel.
